    "summary": "Discover Python's ordered, mutable sequence type: lists, and how to access elements.",
    "content": "# Lesson 21: Introduction to Lists: Creation, Indexing & Mutability\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 21** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Introduction to Lists: Creation, Indexing & Mutability** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nDiscover Python's ordered, mutable sequence type: lists, and how to access elements.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ntech_stack = [\"Python\", \"FastAPI\", \"PostgreSQL\", \"Docker\"]\nprint(\"First tech:\", tech_stack[0])\nprint(\"Last tech:\", tech_stack[-1])\ntech_stack[1] = \"Django\"  # Mutated in-place!\nprint(\"Updated stack:\", tech_stack)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Create a list of 5 course subjects, replace the 3rd subject with 'Data Science', and print the list length.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
