    "summary": "Check item existence, locate first matching indices, and tally frequencies in lists.",
    "content": "# Lesson 24: Searching & Counting in Lists: in, .index(), and .count()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 24** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Searching & Counting in Lists: in, .index(), and .count()** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nCheck item existence, locate first matching indices, and tally frequencies in lists.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ngrades = [\"A\", \"B\", \"A\", \"C\", \"A\", \"B\"]\nprint(\"Count of A grades:\", grades.count(\"A\"))\nprint(\"First B at index:\", grades.index(\"B\"))\nprint(\"Is F present?\", \"F\" in grades)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Analyze a list of server status codes (200, 404, 500) and count how many times 200 appears.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
