    "difficulty": "Beginner",
    "summary": "Sort numbers and strings in ascending, descending, or custom key orders.",
    "content": "# Lesson 25: Ordering Lists: .sort() in-place vs sorted() builtin\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 25** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Ordering Lists: .sort() in-place vs sorted() builtin** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nSort numbers and strings in ascending, descending, or custom key orders.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nnumbers = [45, 12, 89, 3, 27]\nnumbers.sort() # In-place mutation\nprint(\"Sorted in-place:\", numbers)\n\nwords = [\"banana\", \"pie\", \"apple\", \"strawberry\"]\nsorted_by_len = sorted(words, key=len, reverse=True)\nprint(\"Sorted by length:\", sorted_by_len)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Sort a list of student exam scores in descending order and extract the top 3 scores.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
