    "summary": "Pair elements from multiple collections cleanly without index lookups.",
    "content": "# Lesson 29: The zip() Function: Iterating Multiple Iterables in Parallel\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 29** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **The zip() Function: Iterating Multiple Iterables in Parallel** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nPair elements from multiple collections cleanly without index lookups.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nnames = [\"Mohammed\", \"Khalid\", \"Fatima\"]\nscores = [95, 88, 92]\ndepartments = [\"IT\", \"Engineering\", \"Data\"]\n\nfor name, score, dept in zip(names, scores, departments):\n    print(f\"{name} ({dept}): {score} pts\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Pair a list of product names with a list of stock quantities and prices, calculating individual inventory values.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
