    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Mutate dictionaries dynamically by adding new keys, updating existing entries, and removing elements.",
    "content": "# Lesson 37: Modifying Dictionaries: Adding, Updating, .pop(), and del\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 37** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Modifying Dictionaries: Adding, Updating, .pop(), and del** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nMutate dictionaries dynamically by adding new keys, updating existing entries, and removing elements.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ninventory = {\"P-101\": 50, \"P-102\": 30}\ninventory[\"P-103\"] = 85           # Add new\ninventory[\"P-101\"] += 10          # Update\nremoved_qty = inventory.pop(\"P-102\") # Remove\nprint(\"Updated Inventory:\", inventory)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Build a dictionary of stock tickers and prices; allow user to update an existing ticker's price.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
