    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Build an interactive gradebook system storing student records in dictionaries with automated averages.",
    "content": "# Lesson 41: Mini-Project 2: Student Gradebook & Course Average Calculator\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 41** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Mini-Project 2: Student Gradebook & Course Average Calculator** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nBuild an interactive gradebook system storing student records in dictionaries with automated averages.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ngradebook = {\n    \"Mohammed\": [95, 92, 98],\n    \"Sara\": [88, 91, 85],\n    \"Khalid\": [72, 78, 80]\n}\nfor student, scores in gradebook.items():\n    avg = sum(scores) / len(scores)\n    print(f\"📊 {student}: Average = {avg:.1f} ({'Pass' if avg >= 75 else 'Review'})\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Add functionality to calculate the overall class average and identify the top-performing student.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
