    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Encapsulate logic into reusable functions with explicit inputs and outputs.",
    "content": "# Lesson 42: Defining Functions with def, Parameters, and Return Values\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 42** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Defining Functions with def, Parameters, and Return Values** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nEncapsulate logic into reusable functions with explicit inputs and outputs.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_tax(subtotal, tax_rate=0.15):\n    \"\"\"Calculate total sales tax for a transaction.\"\"\"\n    return subtotal * tax_rate\n\nbill = 200.0\ntax = calculate_tax(bill)\nprint(f\"Subtotal: ${bill} | Tax (15%): ${tax} | Grand Total: ${bill + tax}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write a function convert_temperature(temp, to_celsius=True) that converts between Fahrenheit and Celsius.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
