    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Master function argument patterns and avoid the mutable default argument trap.",
    "content": "# Lesson 43: Positional vs Keyword Arguments & Default Parameters\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 43** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Positional vs Keyword Arguments & Default Parameters** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nMaster function argument patterns and avoid the mutable default argument trap.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ndef create_user(username, email, role=\"Student\", is_active=True):\n    return {\"user\": username, \"email\": email, \"role\": role, \"active\": is_active}\n\n# Keyword invocation\nu1 = create_user(email=\"sara@test.com\", username=\"sara\")\nprint(u1)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write a function send_notification(user, message, channel='Email', priority='Normal') and test varied calls.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
