    "content": "# Lesson 48: Docstrings & Python Documentation Standards (PEP 257)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 48** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Docstrings & Python Documentation Standards (PEP 257)** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nWrite professional multi-line docstrings detailing arguments, returns, and raised exceptions.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ndef calculate_bmi(weight_kg: float, height_m: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the Body Mass Index (BMI).\n\n    Args:\n        weight_kg: Weight in kilograms.\n        height_m: Height in meters.\n\n    Returns:\n        The calculated BMI rounded to 2 decimals.\n    \"\"\"\n    return round(weight_kg / (height_m ** 2), 2)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write a fully documented function for calculating compound interest with complete docstrings.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
