    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Construct optimized dictionaries and unique sets with comprehension syntax.",
    "content": "# Lesson 55: Dictionary & Set Comprehensions\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 55** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Dictionary & Set Comprehensions** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nConstruct optimized dictionaries and unique sets with comprehension syntax.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nproducts = [\"Laptop\", \"Mouse\", \"Keyboard\"]\nprices = [1200, 45, 95]\n\nprice_catalog = {p: c for p, c in zip(products, prices)}\nprint(\"Catalog:\", price_catalog)\n\n# Set comprehension\nsquares_set = {x % 5 for x in range(20)}\nprint(\"Unique Remainders:\", squares_set)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Given a dictionary of item: prices, create a new dictionary of only items priced above $50 with a 10% tax added.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
