    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Generate pseudorandom numbers, select lottery winners, and shuffle decks.",
    "content": "# Lesson 57: The random Module: Seeds, randint(), choice(), and shuffle()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 57** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **The random Module: Seeds, randint(), choice(), and shuffle()** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nGenerate pseudorandom numbers, select lottery winners, and shuffle decks.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport random\nprint(\"Random roll (1-6):\", random.randint(1, 6))\n\nprizes = [\"MacBook Pro\", \"iPad Air\", \"Mechanical Keyboard\", \"T-Shirt\"]\nwinner_prize = random.choice(prizes)\nprint(\"Selected Prize:\", winner_prize)\n\ncards = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\nrandom.shuffle(cards)\nprint(\"Shuffled:\", cards)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Build a simple lottery number picker that chooses 6 unique random numbers between 1 and 49.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
