    "summary": "Convert between datetime objects and human-readable string formats.",
    "content": "# Lesson 59: Formatting Dates with strftime() and Parsing with strptime()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 59** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Formatting Dates with strftime() and Parsing with strptime()** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nConvert between datetime objects and human-readable string formats.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime\nnow = datetime.now()\nformatted = now.strftime(\"%A, %d %B %Y - %I:%M %p\")\nprint(\"Human Readable:\", formatted)\n\n# Parsing\ndate_str = \"2026-11-14\"\nparsed_date = datetime.strptime(date_str, \"%Y-%m-%d\")\nprint(\"Parsed year:\", parsed_date.year)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Parse an ISO date string '2026-12-31' and reformat it as 'Dec 31, 2026'.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
