    "title": "Lesson 62: Handling Exceptions with try, except, else, and finally",
    "slug": "python-lesson-62-handling-exceptions-with-try-except-else-and-finally",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Build bulletproof programs that recover gracefully from failures.",
    "content": "# Lesson 62: Handling Exceptions with try, except, else, and finally\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 62** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Handling Exceptions with try, except, else, and finally** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nBuild bulletproof programs that recover gracefully from failures.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ndef safe_divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        res = a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return \"Cannot divide by zero!\"\n    except TypeError:\n        return \"Both arguments must be numbers!\"\n    else:\n        return f\"Calculation succeeded: {res}\"\n    finally:\n        print(\"Execution completed.\")\n\nprint(safe_divide(10, 2))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Wrap user input in a try-except block that insists on receiving a valid integer before proceeding.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 62, Programming, Intermediate",
