    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Enforce business rules by raising built-in exceptions when parameters violate constraints.",
    "content": "# Lesson 63: Raising Exceptions Manually with raise & Custom Validation\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 63** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Raising Exceptions Manually with raise & Custom Validation** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nEnforce business rules by raising built-in exceptions when parameters violate constraints.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ndef register_student(name, age):\n    if not name or len(name) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Student name must be at least 2 characters.\")\n    if age < 16:\n        raise ValueError(\"Student must be at least 16 years old.\")\n    return f\"Student {name} registered successfully.\"\n\ntry:\n    print(register_student(\"A\", 14))\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(\"Registration Failed:\", e)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write a bank withdrawal function that raises a custom ValueError if withdrawal amount exceeds current balance.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 63, Programming, Intermediate",
