    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Create custom domain-specific exception hierarchies inheriting from Exception.",
    "content": "# Lesson 64: Defining Custom Exception Classes\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 64** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Defining Custom Exception Classes** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nCreate custom domain-specific exception hierarchies inheriting from Exception.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass InsufficientFundsError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Raised when an account balance is insufficient for withdrawal.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, balance, amount):\n        super().__init__(f\"Attempted to withdraw ${amount}, but balance is only ${balance}.\")\n        self.balance = balance\n        self.amount = amount\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Define an InvalidPasswordError exception with requirements information and test raising it.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
