    "summary": "Inspect and read contents from text files using Python's built-in file primitives.",
    "content": "# Lesson 65: Reading Text Files: open(), read(), readline(), and readlines()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 65** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Reading Text Files: open(), read(), readline(), and readlines()** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nInspect and read contents from text files using Python's built-in file primitives.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\n# Writing sample file in memory/demo\nsample_content = \"\"\"Line 1: System Online\nLine 2: Server Riyadh\nLine 3: Status 200\"\"\"\n\nlines = sample_content.splitlines()\nfor idx, line in enumerate(lines, 1):\n    print(f\"[{idx}] {line}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Simulate reading an application log file and print lines that contain the keyword 'WARNING'.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
