    "summary": "Avoid memory leaks and file locks using the standard 'with' context manager statement.",
    "content": "# Lesson 66: Safe File Handling with Context Managers (with open(...) as f:)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 66** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Safe File Handling with Context Managers (with open(...) as f:)** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nAvoid memory leaks and file locks using the standard 'with' context manager statement.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\n# Standard idiom for safe file writing\n# with open('log.txt', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n#     f.write(\"Log initialized\\n\")\nprint(\"File context manager automatically ensures f.close() is called even on crash.\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Explain why using 'with open()' is superior to manual 'f = open()' and 'f.close()'.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
