    "summary": "Parse structured tabular CSV data with csv.reader and write rows with csv.writer.",
    "content": "# Lesson 68: Working with CSV Files using the csv Module (reader & writer)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 68** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Working with CSV Files using the csv Module (reader & writer)** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nParse structured tabular CSV data with csv.reader and write rows with csv.writer.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport csv\nimport io\n\ndata = \"\"\"ID,Product,Price,Stock\nP-101,Dell Laptop,1200,15\nP-102,Keyboard,95,60\"\"\"\n\nf = io.StringIO(data)\nreader = csv.reader(f)\nheader = next(reader)\nfor row in reader:\n    print(f\"Product: {row[1]} -> ${row[2]} (Stock: {row[3]})\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Process a CSV string of employee records and calculate the average salary across all rows.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
