    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Access CSV columns by header names instead of error-prone numeric indices.",
    "content": "# Lesson 69: Using csv.DictReader and csv.DictWriter for Column Mapping\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 69** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Using csv.DictReader and csv.DictWriter for Column Mapping** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nAccess CSV columns by header names instead of error-prone numeric indices.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport csv\nimport io\n\ncsv_raw = \"\"\"Name,Role,Hours\nMohammed,Lead Developer,40\nSara,UI Designer,35\"\"\"\n\nf = io.StringIO(csv_raw)\ndict_reader = csv.DictReader(f)\nfor row in dict_reader:\n    print(f\"{row['Name']} works as {row['Role']} ({row['Hours']} hrs)\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Convert a list of user dictionaries into a formatted CSV string using csv.DictWriter.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
