    "summary": "Serialize Python dictionaries to JSON text and parse JSON payloads from web APIs.",
    "content": "# Lesson 70: Working with JSON: json.loads() and json.dumps() for Strings\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 70** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Working with JSON: json.loads() and json.dumps() for Strings** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nSerialize Python dictionaries to JSON text and parse JSON payloads from web APIs.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\npayload = {\n    \"status\": \"success\",\n    \"user_id\": 1044,\n    \"permissions\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"admin\"],\n    \"verified\": True\n}\n# Serialize to formatted JSON string\njson_str = json.dumps(payload, indent=2)\nprint(\"Serialized JSON:\\n\", json_str)\n\n# Parse back to Python dict\nparsed = json.loads(json_str)\nprint(\"Parsed user_id:\", parsed[\"user_id\"])\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Parse a mock weather API JSON string and extract temperature, humidity, and weather description.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
