    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Manipulate cross-platform directory paths cleanly without messy string concatenation.",
    "content": "# Lesson 72: Modern Path Handling with pathlib.Path\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 72** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Modern Path Handling with pathlib.Path** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nManipulate cross-platform directory paths cleanly without messy string concatenation.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# Constructing paths with the / operator\np = Path(\"workspace\") / \"projects\" / \"app.py\"\nprint(\"Path:\", p)\nprint(\"File name:\", p.name)\nprint(\"Stem (no ext):\", p.stem)\nprint(\"Extension:\", p.suffix)\nprint(\"Parent directory:\", p.parent)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Take an arbitrary file path and print its parent folder, filename, and extension using pathlib.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
