    "summary": "Scan directories, filter files by pattern, and create nested directories with Path.mkdir().",
    "content": "# Lesson 73: Directory Operations: Listing, Creating Folders & glob()\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 73** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Directory Operations: Listing, Creating Folders & glob()** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nScan directories, filter files by pattern, and create nested directories with Path.mkdir().\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nprint(\"Pathlib provides p.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\")\nprint(\"Pathlib provides p.glob('*.py') for lightning-fast file discovery.\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write a function that counts how many files in a target directory have a given extension.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
