    "summary": "Clean messy documents, remove sensitive tokens, and reformat dates using regex replacements.",
    "content": "# Lesson 75: Regex Substitution with re.sub() for Data Sanitization\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 75** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Regex Substitution with re.sub() for Data Sanitization** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nClean messy documents, remove sensitive tokens, and reformat dates using regex replacements.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport re\n\nraw_phone = \"(050) 123-4567\"\nclean_digits = re.sub(r'\\D', '', raw_phone)\nprint(\"Clean Digits Only:\", clean_digits)\n\ncensored = re.sub(r'\\d{4}', '****', \"Card: 4111-2222-3333-4444\")\nprint(\"Censored:\", censored)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Sanitize an HTML string by stripping all tags using re.sub(r'<[^>]+>', '', html_text).\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
