    "summary": "Transition from procedural code to Object-Oriented Programming principles.",
    "content": "# Lesson 76: Introduction to OOP: Classes, Objects, and Real-World Modeling\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 76** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Introduction to OOP: Classes, Objects, and Real-World Modeling** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nTransition from procedural code to Object-Oriented Programming principles.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass Student:\n    pass  # Empty class blueprint\n\ns1 = Student()\ns1.name = \"Mohammed\"\nprint(f\"Created student instance: {s1.name} of type {type(s1)}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Create a Car blueprint class, instantiate 2 separate vehicles, and assign custom color attributes to each.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
