    "id": "py-78",
    "lesson_number": 78,
    "title": "Lesson 78: Instance Methods & Mutating Object State",
    "slug": "python-lesson-78-instance-methods-mutating-object-state",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Define behaviors on classes that read and modify the instance's internal variables.",
    "content": "# Lesson 78: Instance Methods & Mutating Object State\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 78** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Instance Methods & Mutating Object State** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nDefine behaviors on classes that read and modify the instance's internal variables.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass BankAccount:\n    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0.0):\n        self.owner = owner\n        self.balance = balance\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        if amount > 0:\n            self.balance += amount\n            return f\"Deposited ${amount}. New balance: ${self.balance}\"\n        return \"Invalid amount.\"\n\n    def withdraw(self, amount):\n        if 0 < amount <= self.balance:\n            self.balance -= amount\n            return f\"Withdrew ${amount}. Remaining: ${self.balance}\"\n        return \"Insufficient funds.\"\n\nacc = BankAccount(\"Mohammed\", 500.0)\nprint(acc.deposit(250.0))\nprint(acc.withdraw(100.0))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Add an apply_interest(rate) method to the BankAccount class that multiplies balance by (1 + rate).\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 78, Programming, Intermediate",
    "read_time": 5,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 186,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #312e81, #8b5cf6)",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-14 12:00:00"
