    "title": "Lesson 79: Instance Attributes vs Class Attributes",
    "slug": "python-lesson-79-instance-attributes-vs-class-attributes",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Intermediate",
    "summary": "Distinguish variables unique to an instance from variables shared across all instances.",
    "content": "# Lesson 79: Instance Attributes vs Class Attributes\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 79** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Instance Attributes vs Class Attributes** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nDistinguish variables unique to an instance from variables shared across all instances.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass Employee:\n    company_name = \"Bargisy EdTech\" # Class attribute shared by all\n\n    def __init__(self, name, salary):\n        self.name = name            # Instance attribute unique to each\n        self.salary = salary\n\ne1 = Employee(\"Sara\", 8000)\ne2 = Employee(\"Khalid\", 8500)\nprint(f\"{e1.name} works at {e1.company_name}\")\nprint(f\"{e2.name} works at {e2.company_name}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Add a class attribute total_employees that increments each time a new Employee instance is initialized.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 79, Programming, Intermediate",
    "read_time": 5,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 183,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #312e81, #8b5cf6)",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-14 12:00:00"
