    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Protect internal data integrity using naming conventions and Python's name mangling.",
    "content": "# Lesson 81: Encapsulation: Private Attributes (_protected and __private)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 81** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Encapsulation: Private Attributes (_protected and __private)** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nProtect internal data integrity using naming conventions and Python's name mangling.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass UserAccount:\n    def __init__(self, username, password):\n        self.username = username\n        self.__password_hash = hash(password) # Private\n\n    def verify_password(self, pwd):\n        return self.__password_hash == hash(pwd)\n\nu = UserAccount(\"admin\", \"secret123\")\nprint(\"User:\", u.username)\nprint(\"Password valid?\", u.verify_password(\"secret123\"))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Demonstrate how Python mangles __private variables by inspecting the object's __dict__.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 81, Programming, Advanced",
    "read_time": 5,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 177,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #312e81, #8b5cf6)",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-14 12:00:00"
