    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Override inherited methods while selectively executing parent behavior with super().",
    "content": "# Lesson 84: Method Overriding & Calling super() in Subclasses\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 84** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Method Overriding & Calling super() in Subclasses** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nOverride inherited methods while selectively executing parent behavior with super().\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass Animal:\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"Generic animal sound\"\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"Woof! Woof!\"\n\nclass Cat(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"Meow!\"\n\npets = [Dog(), Cat(), Animal()]\nfor p in pets:\n    print(p.speak())\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Create a Shape base class with area() returning 0, and Rectangle and Circle subclasses overriding area().\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
