    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Integrate your custom classes with Python's native operators and built-in functions.",
    "content": "# Lesson 86: Magic Methods (Dunder Methods): __len__, __getitem__, and __eq__\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 86** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Magic Methods (Dunder Methods): __len__, __getitem__, and __eq__** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nIntegrate your custom classes with Python's native operators and built-in functions.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass CourseBatch:\n    def __init__(self, name, students):\n        self.name = name\n        self.students = students\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.students)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, index):\n        return self.students[index]\n\nbatch = CourseBatch(\"Cohort 2026\", [\"Mohammed\", \"Sara\", \"Khalid\"])\nprint(\"Batch size with len():\", len(batch))\nprint(\"First student with [0]:\", batch[0])\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Implement __eq__ on a Point(x, y) class so that two points with matching coordinates compare equal with ==.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
