    "summary": "Allow your custom objects to use mathematical operators using __add__, __sub__, and __mul__.",
    "content": "# Lesson 87: Operator Overloading: Customizing +, -, and * for Custom Classes\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 87** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Operator Overloading: Customizing +, -, and * for Custom Classes** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nAllow your custom objects to use mathematical operators using __add__, __sub__, and __mul__.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass Money:\n    def __init__(self, amount, currency=\"USD\"):\n        self.amount = amount\n        self.currency = currency\n\n    def __add__(self, other):\n        if self.currency != other.currency:\n            raise ValueError(\"Currencies must match!\")\n        return Money(self.amount + other.amount, self.currency)\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"${self.amount:.2f} {self.currency}\"\n\nm1 = Money(150.0)\nm2 = Money(85.5)\ntotal = m1 + m2\nprint(\"Summed Money:\", total)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Implement __mul__ on a Vector(x, y) class to perform scalar multiplication (Vector * scalar).\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 87, Programming, Advanced",
