    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Create alternative constructors and utility methods that do not depend on instance state.",
    "content": "# Lesson 88: Class Methods (@classmethod) & Static Methods (@staticmethod)\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 88** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Class Methods (@classmethod) & Static Methods (@staticmethod)** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nCreate alternative constructors and utility methods that do not depend on instance state.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nclass DateHelper:\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_weekend(day_name):\n        return day_name.lower() in [\"friday\", \"saturday\"]\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_string(cls, date_str):\n        # Alternative constructor\n        y, m, d = map(int, date_str.split(\"-\"))\n        return cls()\n\nprint(\"Is Friday weekend?\", DateHelper.is_weekend(\"Friday\"))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Create an Employee class with a class method from_json(json_str) that parses JSON and instantiates the employee.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
