    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Save gigabytes of RAM by replacing square brackets with parentheses for lazy evaluation.",
    "content": "# Lesson 91: Generator Expressions vs List Comprehensions\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 91** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Generator Expressions vs List Comprehensions** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nSave gigabytes of RAM by replacing square brackets with parentheses for lazy evaluation.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n# List comprehension builds entire list in RAM\nlst = [x for x in range(1000)]\n# Generator expression evaluates lazily on demand\ngen = (x for x in range(1000))\n\nprint(\"List memory (bytes):\", sys.getsizeof(lst))\nprint(\"Generator memory (bytes):\", sys.getsizeof(gen))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Sum the squares of numbers from 1 to 1,000,000 using a generator expression inside sum().\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
