    "title": "Lesson 92: Decorators Part 1: Writing Custom Function Wrappers",
    "slug": "python-lesson-92-decorators-part-1-writing-custom-function-wrappers",
    "category": "programming",
    "track": "python",
    "track_name": "Python Path",
    "category_name": "Python Programming",
    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Modify or enhance the behavior of existing functions without changing their source code.",
    "content": "# Lesson 92: Decorators Part 1: Writing Custom Function Wrappers\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 92** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Decorators Part 1: Writing Custom Function Wrappers** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nModify or enhance the behavior of existing functions without changing their source code.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\ndef log_execution(func):\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        print(f\"⚡ [LOG] Calling function '{func.__name__}'...\")\n        res = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        print(f\"✓ [LOG] '{func.__name__}' returned: {res}\")\n        return res\n    return wrapper\n\n@log_execution\ndef calculate_payout(hours, rate):\n    return hours * rate\n\ncalculate_payout(40, 50)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write an @audit_log decorator that prints the arguments passed to any wrapped function.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
    "tags": "Python, Lesson 92, Programming, Advanced",
    "read_time": 5,
    "author": "Mohammed Bargisy",
    "status": "published",
    "views": 144,
    "cover_gradient": "linear-gradient(135deg, #312e81, #8b5cf6)",
