    "difficulty": "Advanced",
    "summary": "Cache expensive function return values automatically to achieve 100x speedups.",
    "content": "# Lesson 94: The functools Module: lru_cache for Memoization & Optimization\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 94** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **The functools Module: lru_cache for Memoization & Optimization** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nCache expensive function return values automatically to achieve 100x speedups.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=128)\ndef fib(n):\n    if n < 2:\n        return n\n    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n\nprint(\"Fibonacci 35 with instant memoization:\", fib(35))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Benchmark recursive Fibonacci with and without @lru_cache for n=30 to observe the performance difference.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
