    "summary": "Harness specialized container data types for cleaner, faster code.",
    "content": "# Lesson 95: The collections Module: Counter, defaultdict, and namedtuple\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 95** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **The collections Module: Counter, defaultdict, and namedtuple** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nHarness specialized container data types for cleaner, faster code.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\n# Frequency counter\nwords = [\"python\", \"excel\", \"python\", \"code\", \"excel\", \"python\"]\ncounts = Counter(words)\nprint(\"Most common:\", counts.most_common(2))\n\n# Defaultdict with list default\ngroups = defaultdict(list)\ngroups[\"developers\"].append(\"Mohammed\")\ngroups[\"designers\"].append(\"Sara\")\nprint(\"Group dict:\", dict(groups))\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Use Counter to count character frequency in a paragraph and display the top 3 most common letters.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
