    "summary": "Avoid unexpected mutations when copying complex nested data structures.",
    "content": "# Lesson 97: Deep Copy vs Shallow Copy with the copy Module\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 97** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Deep Copy vs Shallow Copy with the copy Module** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nAvoid unexpected mutations when copying complex nested data structures.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport copy\n\noriginal = [1, [2, 3], 4]\nshallow = list(original)\ndeep = copy.deepcopy(original)\n\noriginal[1][0] = 999\nprint(\"Original:\", original)\nprint(\"Shallow (mutated):\", shallow)\nprint(\"Deep (protected):\", deep)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Demonstrate the danger of shallow copy on a dictionary containing nested lists.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
