    "summary": "Write robust automated assertions to test function correctness and catch regressions.",
    "content": "# Lesson 99: Automated Unit Testing with the unittest Standard Module\n\nWelcome to **Lesson 99** of the Bargisy eSchool Complete Python Curriculum. In this lesson, you will master **Automated Unit Testing with the unittest Standard Module** with practical explanations and live code examples.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concepts & Theoretical Background\nWrite robust automated assertions to test function correctness and catch regressions.\n\nIn modern software development and automation, mastering this concept gives you the ability to write clean, maintainable, and high-performance Python code.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practical Code Implementation\nStudy the code snippet below and run it in the **Python Code Lab**:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nclass TestMathOperations(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_positive_addition(self):\n        self.assertEqual(add(10, 5), 15)\n\n    def test_negative_addition(self):\n        self.assertEqual(add(-2, -3), -5)\n\n# Test suite runner demo\nsuite = unittest.TestLoader().loadTestsFromTestCase(TestMathOperations)\nrunner = unittest.TextTestRunner(verbosity=0)\nres = runner.run(suite)\nprint(\"Tests Passed:\", res.wasSuccessful())\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **Pro Tip for Python Developers**: Always adhere to PEP 8 standards and keep your code expressive and readable. In Python, explicit is better than implicit!\n\n---\n\n## 3. Hands-on Student Challenge\nTake your knowledge to the next level by completing this coding challenge:\n1. **Objective**: Write unit tests for a sanitize_email(email) function verifying lowercase conversion and whitespace stripping.\n2. Open the **Interactive Python Lab** in Bargisy eSchool.\n3. Test your solution, verify the output terminal, and observe how your code behaves!\n",
